---
import LegalPage from '../../components/LegalPage.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import GuideFigure from '../../components/GuideFigure.astro';

const title = 'Framer CMS: add projects and posts';
const description = 'How the Framer CMS works in a template: collections, fields, CMS pages and lists, plan limits, and how to add a project or a blog post without touching the design.';
const url = new URL('/guides/framer-cms/', Astro.site).href;
const harrier = await getEntry('templates', 'harrier');

const steps = [
  ['Open the CMS', 'In your Framer project, open the CMS panel. Each collection the template uses is listed there, for example Projects or Blog.'],
  ['Open a collection', 'A collection is a table: each row is an item (one project, one post), each column a field (title, cover, text, date).'],
  ['Replace a sample item', 'Click a sample item and change its fields. Start by editing one item and check how it looks on the site before you do the rest.'],
  ['Add your own items', 'Add a new item and fill every field. Its page is created from the CMS page design, so it matches the others.'],
  ['Delete the samples', 'Remove the sample items you do not use, so no placeholder reaches the live site.'],
  ['Publish', 'Publish the site. New and changed items go live with it.'],
];
---

<LegalPage accent="posts"
  kicker="Guide"
  title={title}
  seoTitle="Framer CMS explained: how to add projects and blog posts"
  description={description}
  updated="November 3, 2026"
  jsonLd={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'How to add content with the Framer CMS',
    description,
    url,
    step: steps.map(([name, text], i) => ({ '@type': 'HowToStep', position: i + 1, name, text })),
  }}
>
  <p>
    The Framer CMS keeps repeating content, like projects, blog posts or team members, in tables instead of on the
    page. You add a row, and the site shows it in the right place with the right design. It is how a template stays
    tidy when your portfolio grows from three projects to thirty.
  </p>

  <h2>Four words to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Collection:</strong> one kind of content, such as Projects. A template can have several.</li>
    <li><strong>Item:</strong> one entry in a collection, such as one project.</li>
    <li><strong>Field:</strong> one piece of an item: title, image, text, date, link and so on.</li>
    <li><strong>CMS page:</strong> the one design used for every item's own page, such as /work/my-project.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Lists of items on normal pages, such as the grid of projects on Work, are filled from a collection too. You set
    how many to show, in which order and with which filter.
  </p>

  {harrier && <GuideFigure template={harrier} picture={1} caption="Harrier: blog posts and changelog entries come from two CMS collections" />}

  <h2>Add a project or a post</h2>
  <ol>
    {steps.map(([name, text]) => <li><strong>{name}.</strong> {text}</li>)}
  </ol>

  <h2>Tips that save time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keep images the same shape.</strong> Covers with the same aspect ratio keep grids even.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the slug yourself.</strong> The slug is the item's address. Short and readable is better for people and search.</li>
    <li><strong>Fill the SEO fields</strong> if the template has them, so each item gets its own title and description in search.</li>
    <li><strong>Import from a spreadsheet.</strong> Moving many items from another tool? Collections can be filled from a CSV file.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Plan limits</h2>
  <p>
    CMS limits depend on the Framer plan of the published site. On November 3, 2026: Basic allows 2 collections and
    1,000 items, Pro 10 collections and 2,500 items. You can build with more on the free plan, and only hit the limit
    when you publish on a paid plan. Details are in <a href="/guides/framer-pricing/">Framer pricing explained</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The CMS in our templates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/templates/ethan-walker/">Ethan Walker</a>: Projects, Services and Privacy Policy. Step-by-step in <a href="/guides/framer-portfolio-template/">the portfolio walkthrough</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="/templates/softic-studio/">Softic Studio</a>: Projects and Team.</li>
    <li><a href="/templates/harrier/">Harrier</a>: Blog and Changelog.</li>
    <li><a href="/templates/alpine-app/">Alpine App</a> and <a href="/templates/sanctun/">Sanctun</a> have no CMS: everything is edited on the page.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="mt-xl flex flex-wrap gap-sm">
    <Button href="/templates/free/" arrow>Free templates</Button>
    <Button href="/guides/" variant="secondary" arrow>More guides</Button>
  </div>
</LegalPage>
